# Line endings.
#
# Git for Windows sets core.autocrlf=true system-wide, so on a Windows
# checkout every text file arrives with CRLF. Two things follow:
#
#  * "text=auto" stores LF in the repository whatever the working tree holds,
#    so a file edited on Windows comes back as a normal small diff instead of
#    a whole-file rewrite. (Do not run "git add --renormalize ." to enforce
#    this on the rest of the tree: a few upstream files are stored with CRLF
#    and converting them would produce a huge diff against upstream for no
#    gain. Leave them until something actually edits them.)
#
#  * The shell scripts must check out with LF or they will not run under Git
#    Bash, WSL, macOS or Linux, so they are pinned; the Windows scripts are
#    pinned the other way.

* text=auto

gradlew text eol=lf
dev      text eol=lf
*.sh     text eol=lf

*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
